.TH pg 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
pg \- page through a file, forwards and back
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pg
[\fIfile\fR ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B pg
shows each
.I file
twenty lines at a time, clearing the screen between pages.  With no
.IR file ,
it reads standard input.
.PP
What it has over
.BR more (1)
is that it can go
.I back:
it keeps the last 64 page positions in a 16K ring, so anything that has
scrolled off recently can be looked at again.
.SH KEYS
It gives no prompt.  At the foot of a page it waits for one character.
.TP
newline
The next page.
.TP
.B \-
The previous page.  Also control-underscore.
.TP
.B /
The oldest page still remembered.  Also control-Y.
.TP
.B !
Start a shell.  Leaving the shell comes back to the same page.
.PP
There is no quit key.  Interrupt it, or page to the end.
.SH NOTES
Keys are read from
.I standard error,
not standard input, which is what lets
.B pg
page a pipe.  It follows that stderr must be the terminal:
.B pg
with stderr redirected reads nothing and stops.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR more (1),
.BR vi (1),
.BR man (1)
